---
title: Transparent Use of County Resources
order: 4
icon: scale
---

Corruption has stolen 30 billion shillings from Nairobi's roads, schools, and clinics in the last five years — money budgeted, allocated, and vanished. Audit reports gather dust on shelves while residents of Kahawa West and Dandora navigate cratered roads and wait for services that were paid for and never delivered. The budget process is opaque, procurement is rigged, and the public is locked out of decisions that shape their daily lives.

We will implement participatory budgeting starting at the ward level. Before the County Assembly approves any budget, the county will hold public hearings in every ward — in plain language, not bureaucratic jargon — showing exactly how much is allocated to each project, who is implementing it, and when it will be completed. Every procurement document — tender notices, bid evaluations, contracts, and completion certificates — will be published on a public portal within seven days of award.

An independent county audit office, reporting directly to the public — not to the county executive — will conduct quarterly audits and publish findings within 30 days. The County Oversight Committee, composed of civil society representatives, professional bodies, and religious leaders, will have the power to summon any county official and compel production of documents. Whistleblowers who expose corruption will be protected by county law — not victimised, not silenced.

The Nairobi County government spends your money. You have a right to know how. We will enact a County Transparency Ordinance making proactive disclosure the default — budgets, expenditures, contracts, project status, payroll, and development plans all online in open, machine-readable formats. A transparent county is a functional county. A corrupt county is a failed county. Under Dr. Bill Llia's leadership, Nairobi will choose transparency — and never look back.
